# Spec-driven development with Specgate

This project was generated ready for
[Specgate](https://rsaglobaltech.github.io/specgate/): requirements with
scenarios, tests that name them, and a gate that will not call anything done
while it is red.

Lost at any point? Run `npx specgate` on its own: it shows where the
specification stands, and the one command to run next.

## Once

forja ran these for you when it installed the dependencies. If it could not:

```bash
npx specgate adopt --no-capabilities
npx specgate config set test_cmd "npm test"
```

## Each module of the product

1. **Write the brief.** Paste the client's brief into `docs/brief.md` as it
   is, and fill in the four fields at its top (product, surfaces, actors,
   modules). `npx specgate draft` explains each field, and
   `npx specgate draft --agent "claude -p"` lets your agent propose them.
2. **Draft the module.** `npx specgate draft <module> --agent "claude -p"` —
   your agent writes the requirements and scenarios, and Specgate checks them
   against the brief: a value the brief never gave, a missing scenario, a
   question nobody asked. Without an agent, `npx specgate draft <module>`
   prints the line to paste into one.
3. **Repeat `npx specgate draft <module>`** until it says *Ready to review*.
   It ends with **What now**: numbered steps, and where every module stands.
4. **Review it** with whoever knows the business; the questions go to the
   client.
5. **Archive it**: `npx specgate change archive draft-<module>`. The
   requirements join the specification.
6. **Build each requirement, test first.** A test that names `REQ-007` is the
   link<% if (java) { %> — in Java, put it in the test's `@DisplayName`<% } %>.
   `forja add usecase <module>/<Name> --req REQ-007` writes the use case and
   that test for you (without `--req`, it creates a new requirement).
   Then `npx specgate done REQ-007`: it refuses while the suite is red.
7. **Before every pull request:** `npx specgate check`. The generated CI runs
   the same gate.

## Where it meets the architecture

Each module of the specification is a hexagonal module of the code<% if (java) { %>
(`apps/api/.../<module>/`)<% } else { %> (`packages/core/src/<module>/`)<% } %>:
its scenarios are proven by the tests of its use cases and its API.
